x_comment(x_CopperInterconnectsOnSiliconChips,"In 1997, IBM replaced aluminum interconnects with copper in the PowerPC processor, leading to higher speeds due to lower resistivity.  \nThis was a major process change because, while aluminum had been vapor deposited, copper is electrolyticly plated (as on printed circuit holes) and requires a thin liner material to act as a diffusion barrier between the copper and silicon.\nPrimary source: Edelstein et al, \"Full Copper Wiring in a Sub-0.25mm CMOS ULSI Technology\", Technical Digest, IEEE International Electron Devices Meeting, 197, p.773.") :- x_cid(a65b125fff823f).
xc_microtheory(a65b125fff823f,x_MetaengineeringInnovationsMt).
