x_comment(x_WilliamsTube,"The Williams tube is a computer memory in which bits are stored as excited phosphors on a CRT tube.  It was able to read and write bit sequences thousands of times per second.\nThe Williams tube evolved out of British work on radar during WWII, replaced paper tape as a dynamic storage, and was itself largely superseded by ferrite core memory.\nThe tube stored charge on the phosphor, which would leak away on the order of a second.  Williams designed a read and rewrite process called \"regeneration\" that anticipated DRAM.") :- x_cid(a65b125fffae33).
xc_microtheory(a65b125fffae33,x_MetaengineeringInnovationsMt).
